    {
      "id": "e985941a5ee1fb6232fd1f1d4153aa6a4169a5c0",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 419327815,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Yes, I did so and I'll push it... though going through that useful exercise revealed something else. The current code for fragment_add() throws a ReassemblyError exception if it gets a duplicate packet *after* the current reassembly is completed (i.e. more_frags was FALSE and set_partial_reassembly didn't get hit). (add_seq() doesn't do that.) It doesn't set FD_OVERLAP et al. If it gets a duplicate while still reassembling, then it goes through the other code I've been looking at, similar to fragment_add_seq() - except for the problem of only doing it if the new packets is an overlapping one with new bytes, not for duplicates or subsets.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
